{
  // Vaipakam first-party autonomous keeper Worker.
  //
  // Cron-driven only (no fetch handler). Stage 3 PR2 of the
  // Worker split — see docs/DesignsAndPlans/Stage3WorkerSplitPlan.md
  // for the full classification and migration sequencing.
  //
  // Owns: HF watcher loop + autonomous liquidation submission +
  // HF-band-downgrade Telegram / Push alerts + daily oracle
  // snapshot signer (moved from agent in the Stage 3 architectural-
  // rebalance commit; co-located with the existing
  // KEEPER_PRIVATE_KEY consumer so the signing key lives on
  // exactly one Worker, per staging plan §2). LIVE (per §7 of the Stage 3
  // plan; this said "Future scope"): the off-chain offer matcher runs
  // every cron tick and signs matchOffers/matchIntent.
  //
  // Distinct from the public reference keeper-bot at the sibling
  // `vaipakam-keeper-bot` repo (different deploy target, different
  // trust assumptions). That repo follows its own ABI sync + CI
  // workflow per the Phase 9.A docs in CLAUDE.md.
  //
  // Custom domain: not user-facing. Worker name `vaipakam-keeper`
  // is the deploy-switch identifier and stays post-cutover.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "vaipakam-keeper",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-04-23",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],

  // ── KEEPER IS DEPLOYED BUT NOT SCHEDULED (#1896, operator decision) ──
  //
  // The schedule is deliberately EMPTY. Everything else about this
  // Worker is untouched and stays deployed: the script, the D1 binding,
  // all 17 secret / secrets-store / plain-text bindings, and the
  // `KEEPER_ENABLED` secret. Nothing has to be recreated to bring it
  // back — only this list.
  //
  // WHY. Measured on 2026-08-24 (#1896): `outcome: exceededCpu` on
  // ~100% of invocations, cpuTime pinned at the 10 ms ceiling, over the
  // whole retained window. So every minute the Worker was invoked,
  // burned its ceiling, and was terminated rather than finishing.
  //
  // NOT "completed nothing" — an earlier revision said that and the
  // release note was corrected while this comment was not (Codex #1924
  // r3). Passes run concurrently, and several DO emit their completion
  // line before another exhausts the invocation; in an armed
  // configuration those may have sent alerts or written records first.
  // What is certain is the termination, not an absence of work.
  //
  // WHICH pass burns it is NOT established, and an earlier revision of
  // this comment claimed it was (Codex #1924 r1). `watcher` and
  // `liquidityConfidence` are the two with no completion line, but
  // every due pass is started concurrently through `ctx.waitUntil`, so
  // a missing line means only "still in flight when the invocation
  // died" — which a pass merely awaiting an RPC also satisfies. The
  // timing wrapper measures WALL time and says so itself: it can
  // EXCLUDE suspects, not name one. Attribution needs a profile.
  //
  // It also returns a cron trigger to the account pool. The free plan
  // caps triggers at FIVE per ACCOUNT and all five were in use — see
  // `apps/indexer/src/cronRouting.ts`, which records a deploy that
  // failed for exactly that reason.
  //
  // WHAT STOPS THAT WAS NOT ALREADY STOPPED. FOUR passes run ungated by
  // `KEEPER_ENABLED`, so unscheduling does cost function. Two earlier
  // revisions of this comment got this wrong in escalating ways — first
  // claiming it cost nothing, then naming only two of the four (Codex
  // #1924 r1/r2). README §"What the kill-switch does and does not stop
  // (#1548)" is the authority and lists all of them:
  //   - `watcher` — THE BIGGEST LOSS, and the one both earlier revisions
  //     missed. It keeps evaluating positions and keeps sending Telegram
  //     and Push health-factor alerts to USERS. Unscheduling stops those
  //     alerts: a borrower approaching liquidation is no longer told.
  //   - `dailyOracleSnapshot` — gated only on KEEPER_PRIVATE_KEY, and
  //     deliberately so: the same README section records the 2026-08-03
  //     decision that the snapshot is a public good, left ungated
  //     precisely SO THAT disabling the keeper for an unrelated reason
  //     would not leave holes in the oracle series. Unscheduling leaves
  //     exactly those holes, and they do NOT backfill on re-enable — the
  //     contract writes only the current day's slot. Mitigation:
  //     `captureDailyPriceSnapshot` is permissionlessly callable, so any
  //     address can keep the series intact meanwhile.
  //   - `preGraceWatcher` — no arming check at all; borrowers stop
  //     getting the pre-grace warning.
  //   - `liquidityConfidence` — its gate is narrower than it looks: it
  //     decides only whether to SUBMIT on-chain. The pass still reads
  //     and still writes its D1 counter, so unscheduling stops the
  //     confidence state advancing at all.
  //
  // TO RE-ENABLE, once the CPU work in #1896 is done:
  //   1. DISARM FIRST — `( cd apps/keeper && wrangler secret put
  //      KEEPER_ENABLED )`, entering `false` (Codex #1924 r4). Do this
  //      BEFORE restoring the schedule, and do not assume the hold left
  //      it off: the binding still exists on the live Worker (verified
  //      2026-08-24, `secret_text`), a deploy never deletes a secret, and
  //      a secret's VALUE cannot be read back — so "it should be false"
  //      is unverifiable, not a check. If it is in fact `true`, the six
  //      gated passes begin submitting transactions the moment the
  //      restored schedule propagates, which is one step before step 7
  //      would have observed anything.
  //   2. Restore the schedule below (`"crons": ["* * * * *"]`).
  //   3. Confirm a cron slot is free — the cap is five per account.
  //   4. `wrangler deploy --keep-vars` — and the flag is NOT optional
  //      (Codex #1924 r2 P1). Editing this file changes nothing on its
  //      own; a schedule reaches Cloudflare only via a deploy. But a
  //      BARE deploy "will delete all vars before setting those found in
  //      the Wrangler configuration", and this config declares only
  //      `TG_BOT_USERNAME`. Every dashboard-managed tuning override —
  //      `HF_SCALE`, the `LIQ_*` confidence and Tier-3 thresholds,
  //      `SPLIT_MIN_IMPROVEMENT_BPS`, `PARTIAL_LIQ_MIN_HF_BPS` (see
  //      `env.ts`) — would be wiped one step before step 6 arms the
  //      fund-moving passes. That is liquidation behaviour silently
  //      reverting to defaults at the exact moment it starts mattering.
  //      Capture the live vars first if you have any doubt they are all
  //      recoverable.
  //   5. Read the schedule back TRIGGER-AWARE — Settings → Trigger
  //      Events, or query the Worker's `/schedules` endpoint. A
  //      mistyped or wrongly-nested key leaves the previous state in
  //      place, and a quiet `wrangler tail` only proves no tick has
  //      fired YET.
  //   6. Allow the propagation window: Cloudflare documents cron
  //      updates taking up to 15 minutes to reach every location.
  //   7. VALIDATE AN UNARMED TICK FIRST (Codex #1924 r3). With
  //      `KEEPER_ENABLED` set to `false` in step 1, `wrangler tail
  //      vaipakam-keeper` and confirm the outcome is `ok` rather than
  //      `exceededCpu`. Arming before observing a live tick means the
  //      six gated passes can submit transactions on an invocation that
  //      still dies — the CPU fix being incomplete or regressed is
  //      exactly the case this sequence exists for, so it must be ruled
  //      out while the passes that move funds are still inert.
  //      If it still reports `exceededCpu`: set `"crons": []` again and
  //      redeploy, and leave `KEEPER_ENABLED` at `false`. That is the
  //      rollback, and it costs nothing because nothing was armed.
  //   8. Only then set the `KEEPER_ENABLED` secret to `true`. The
  //      fund-moving passes stay inert until it is, and `passIsArmed`
  //      names any binding still blocking each pass, one line per pass
  //      per tick.
  //   9. Tail once more and confirm the ARMED tick also ends `ok`, and
  //      that every due pass emits a `done in Nms` line — an absent
  //      line means the pass was still in flight when the invocation
  //      ended. If not, roll back BOTH halves: `KEEPER_ENABLED` to
  //      `false` first (it is now genuinely armed), then `"crons": []`
  //      and redeploy.
  //  10. Clear any checks DEFERRED during the hold. Specifically: if
  //      `PUSH_CHANNEL_PK` was rotated while the keeper was unscheduled,
  //      `docs/ops/IncidentRunbook.md`'s Push-rotation step 6 could only
  //      be satisfied against the agent — the keeper had no invocation
  //      from which to send. Confirm a `[push] sent channel=…` line from
  //      THIS Worker now, because an incompatible signer would have
  //      stayed invisible for the whole hold.
  //
  // The original cadence note, still true and still the target to
  // restore: the watcher is internally tick-rate-aware — each tick
  // sweeps `getChainConfigs(env)` round-robin and bails early on chains
  // that already have notify_state at the same band since the previous
  // tick. "* * * * *" matched the historical ops/hf-watcher cadence;
  // tighter schedules don't help (HF reads are per-tick rate-limited
  // inside the watcher).
  "triggers": {
    "crons": [],
  },

  // D1 binding — `vaipakam-archive`, the STAGING database for the
  // 3-Worker split. NOT `vaipakam-alerts-db` (primary/production),
  // which this comment used to name — that misdirects migration and
  // incident work at production. The binding below is authoritative.
  //
  // All three Workers bind this same database id and all three WRITE
  // it. A D1 binding is database-scoped, not table-scoped, so the
  // per-Worker table lists below are a source convention, not an
  // enforced boundary (#1722). Tables this Worker touches:
  //   - `user_thresholds`   (per-user-loan HF bands; written by frontend)
  //   - `telegram_links`    (Telegram handshake links; agent writes — but
  //                          THIS Worker deletes from them too:
  //                          `watcher.ts:60` calls `sweepExpiredLinks`,
  //                          which DELETEs expired rows (`db.ts:303`).
  //                          "agent writes" alone read as though the keeper
  //                          only consumed this table)
  //   - `notify_state`,
  //     `hf_band_state`     (per-user notify + HF-band dedupe)
  //   - `pre_grace_notify_state` (pre-grace warning dedupe; read and
  //                          written by the scheduled `runPreGraceWatcher`)
  //   - `indexer_cursor`    (READ ONLY — `recordHfBandNotifications`
  //                          reads the indexer's `notified` watermark and
  //                          `diamond` tip before materializing rows, so a
  //                          cron sentinel can't out-sort still-pending
  //                          event rows. The indexer owns the writes)
  //   - `notifications`     (HF-band downgrade rows, INSERT OR IGNORE on
  //                          the dedup key; same call path, reached from
  //                          the scheduled `runLiquidator`)
  //   - `loans`             (active-loan rows; written by indexer; this
  //                          Worker reads to enumerate live loans)
  //   - `offers`            (read by dailyOracleSnapshot; NOT by the
  //                          matcher, whose discovery is on-chain)
  //   - `oracle_snapshot_state` (daily snapshot dedupe)
  //   - `keeper_commitment_*`, `keeper_remit_ack*`, `liquidity_confidence`
  //                         (commitment scan, remit ack, liquidity probe)
  //
  // Names verified against apps/indexer/migrations/. `thresholds`,
  // `link_codes` and `loan_index_*` were named here and exist under no
  // migration. The three entries above them were verified the other way
  // round — from the live call graph rather than from this list — because
  // a table this Worker touches but does not name is the failure mode a
  // name check cannot catch: an auditor reading only the binding comment
  // would conclude the keeper never writes the user-facing `notifications`
  // inbox, and a migration author would not know to check it.
  // Cross-Worker D1 sharing is the deliberate coupling that lets the
  // three Workers stay independent without a message-bus layer
  // (Stage 3 plan §5).
  // D1 binding — `vaipakam-archive` is the STAGING database for
  // the new 3-Worker split (apps/{keeper,indexer,agent}). It's
  // distinct from `vaipakam-alerts-db` which the legacy
  // `vaipakam-hf-watcher` Worker (primary infra) writes to and
  // is intentionally untouched until staging is fully validated.
  // See docs/DesignsAndPlans/CloudflareStagingDeployPlan.md §3
  // for the staging-vs-primary D1 split.
  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "DB",
      "database_name": "vaipakam-archive",
      "database_id": "3cffebf5-b652-4da7-953c-9e1d143ad2fe",
    },
  ],

  // T-078 — the keeper's secrets are read from the account-level
  // Cloudflare Secrets Store (store `vaipakam-credentials`, id
  // 1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e), not per-Worker
  // `wrangler secret put`. Each binding is resolved asynchronously
  // once per cron tick in `resolveEnv()` (src/env.ts). Local
  // `wrangler dev` needs the same secrets created WITHOUT the
  // `--remote` flag.
  //
  // `RPC_ZKEVM` is omitted (Polygon zkEVM out of scope); `RPC_POLYGON`
  // is agent-only. (#1651: this said `RPC_POLYGON_AMOY` was agent-only
  // too — apps/indexer binds it.) `TG_BOT_TOKEN` must still be
  // created in the store (the `VaipakamBot` token) before deploy.
  "secrets_store_secrets": [
    { "binding": "RPC_BASE",          "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_BASE" },
    { "binding": "RPC_ETH",           "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_ETH" },
    { "binding": "RPC_ARB",           "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_ARB" },
    { "binding": "RPC_OP",            "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_OP" },
    { "binding": "RPC_BNB",           "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_BNB" },
    { "binding": "RPC_SEPOLIA",       "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_SEPOLIA" },
    { "binding": "RPC_BASE_SEPOLIA",  "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_BASE_SEPOLIA" },
    { "binding": "RPC_ARB_SEPOLIA",   "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_ARB_SEPOLIA" },
    { "binding": "RPC_OP_SEPOLIA",    "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_OP_SEPOLIA" },
    { "binding": "RPC_BNB_TESTNET",   "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "RPC_BNB_TESTNET" },
    { "binding": "TG_BOT_TOKEN",      "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "TG_BOT_TOKEN" },
    { "binding": "PUSH_CHANNEL_PK",   "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "PUSH_CHANNEL_PK" },
    { "binding": "ZEROEX_API_KEY",    "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "ZEROEX_API_KEY" },
    { "binding": "ONEINCH_API_KEY",   "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "ONEINCH_API_KEY" },
    { "binding": "KEEPER_PRIVATE_KEY", "store_id": "1e66429d0fa24aa38a27bc05b7bcf63e", "secret_name": "KEEPER_PRIVATE_KEY" },
  ],

  // Operator-managed vars (non-secret config — plain `vars`):
  //   - KEEPER_ENABLED   — 'true' arms the autonomous liquidation /
  //                        matcher / liquidity-confidence submit paths.
  //   - TG_BOT_USERNAME  — public Telegram bot handle.
  //   - REWARD_REMIT_ENABLED — 'true' arms the #776 reward-budget remit pass
  //     (only after the keeper EOA is authorized on-chain) AND the #1222
  //     B2-d2 remit-ACK pass (delivered-backing reservations; needs D1
  //     migration 0044 applied first). Optional knobs:
  //     REWARD_REMIT_LOOKBACK_DAYS (default 45),
  //     REWARD_REMIT_LANE_CAP (wei, default 50000e18 = the on-chain lane default; see #918).
  //   - REWARD_COMMIT_ENABLED — 'true' arms the #1222 B2-d1 mirror→Base
  //     commitment-report pass (mirror chains only; keeper EOA needs on-chain
  //     KEEPER_ROLE). Optional: REWARD_COMMIT_LOOKBACK_DAYS (default 14).
  //   - FRONTEND_ORIGIN, LIQ_* / SPLIT_* / PARTIAL_LIQ_* tuning knobs
  //     (see src/env.ts) — all optional, sensible defaults.
  "vars": {
    // Public Telegram bot handle (the @-name from BotFather). Filled
    // post-provision; the worker accepts an empty string and returns
    // bot_url: null in the handshake response when not configured.
    "TG_BOT_USERNAME": "",
  },

  "observability": {
    "enabled": true,
  },
}
